I checked all three: the man page, the source, and doas itself. They
agree that the arguments must match exactly, so a prefix is not
enough. The jail has security/doas 6.4 installed, which is the port of
Jesse Smith's doas from codeberg.org/thejessesmith/doas.

doas.conf(5), as installed in the jail, says:

    args [argument ...]
                 Arguments to command.  The command arguments provided by the
                 user need to match those specified.  The keyword args alone
                 means that command must be run without any arguments.

The source settles what "match" means. In doas.c, match() (near line
113) compares the rule's arguments with the ones given:

    if (r->cmdargs) {
            /* if arguments were given, they should match explicitly */
            for (i = 0; r->cmdargs[i]; i++) {
                    if (!cmdargs[i])
                            return 0;
                    if (strcmp(r->cmdargs[i], cmdargs[i]))
                            return 0;
            }
            if (cmdargs[i])
                    return 0;
    }

The loop does check the rule's arguments as a prefix of the ones
given. The last test then rejects the rule if any argument is left
over, so you cannot add anything after the prefix. When the rule has
no args at all, this block is skipped and any arguments pass. That is
why a bare "cmd /usr/sbin/pkg" would also allow pkg delete.

Last, doas -C checks a config file against a command line without
running anything. I tested the rule you described with the doas
installed in the jail:

    $ cat doas.conf
    permit nopass claude as root cmd /usr/sbin/pkg args install -r FreeBSD-ports
    $ doas -C doas.conf /usr/sbin/pkg install -r FreeBSD-ports
    permit nopass
    $ doas -C doas.conf /usr/sbin/pkg install -r FreeBSD-ports -y jq
    deny
    $ doas -C doas.conf /usr/sbin/pkg install -r FreeBSD-ports jq
    deny
    $ doas -C doas.conf /usr/sbin/pkg install -r FreeBSD-ports -- jq
    deny

I meant to run the same test against a rule with no args to show pkg
delete being allowed, but the sandbox I run in blocked it. That case
rests on the source above, where no args skips the whole check.

The wrapper script is the only way I found to allow "install from
FreeBSD-ports" with any package names while still blocking everything
else. Writing one rule per package would also work, but then every
new package needs a doas.conf change, which defeats the point.

